---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { config } from "../lib/config";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="FAQ | CANIX402"
  description="Answers to common Canix402 questions: Algorand or Base agents, gateway vs upstream API, 402 preflight behavior, client-side payment signing, USDC asset metadata, and API-only data access."
  keywords="Canix402 FAQ, x402 questions, agent integration, Algorand, Base, USDC asset id, gateway, payment signing"
>
  <h1 class="page-title">FAQ</h1>

  <div class="faq-list">
    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>Should my agent call the gateway or the upstream API directly?</h2>
      <p>
        Use the Caddy gateway URL. x402 enforcement, payment headers, and settlement happen at the
        gateway edge. Discovery/OpenAPI should also be fetched through the gateway in production.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>Why do paid routes return 402 first?</h2>
      <p>
        That is expected. The gateway advertises payment requirements through
        <code>PAYMENT-REQUIRED</code> so your client can sign and retry with
        <code>PAYMENT-SIGNATURE</code>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>Does the website sign payments for me?</h2>
      <p>
        No. Payment signing stays client-side in your agent or wallet integration. This site only
        documents the flow and links to machine-readable contracts.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>Can my agent use Algorand, Base, or both?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. An agent may pay and act on Algorand only, on Base only, or on both. Nothing in
        ranking, eligibility, pricing, or access requires a second chain. Select the payment accept
        by <code>network</code>. Algorand stays first. Filter the catalog with
        <code>chain=algorand</code> or <code>chain=base</code> when you want one chain's rows.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>Do I have to supply a Base address?</h2>
      <p>
        No. An Algorand-only agent is not penalized for omitting a Base address. Wallet routes
        (<code>/positions</code>, <code>/opportunities/personalized</code>, <code>/eligibility</code>,
        <code>/plans</code>, Algorand quotes, and swaps) take an Algorand address and nothing else.
        Leaving out a <code>0x</code> address does not lower rank, fail the request, or block
        payment. A Base address is required only on the call that needs it: a Morpho quote
        (<code>userAddress</code> is <code>0x…</code>) or a payment signed against the Base accept.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>Which USDC asset should I expect?</h2>
      <p>
        Use the asset published on the accept you select. On Algorand mainnet the USDC ASA id is
        <code>31566704</code>. On Base the asset is Circle USDC
        <code>0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913</code>. Confirm both against live
        discovery before signing.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>Can I browse opportunities on this website without paying?</h2>
      <p>
        No. This site is onboarding documentation only. Opportunity data remains API-only behind
        x402 payment gates.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>Which MCP URL should I use?</h2>
      <p>
        Production remote MCP: <code>{config.mcpUrl}</code> (Streamable HTTP).
        Verify with <code>{config.mcpWellKnownUrl}</code>. Discovery and the API x402
        manifest advertise this URL in <code>mcpServer.url</code> / <code>mcpUrl</code>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>Do I need a wallet mnemonic in the MCP server?</h2>
      <p>
        No. The remote MCP is walletless. Your agent or client wallet signs USDC payments and passes
        <code>paymentSignature</code> when retrying paid MCP tools. Never put mnemonics in MCP
        server configuration.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>Should I use MCP or direct HTTP?</h2>
      <p>
        Use <a href="/mcp">MCP</a> when your agent host supports MCP tools (Cursor, Claude Desktop).
        Use direct HTTP when building a custom client against the Caddy gateway. Both hit the same
        public gateway for paid data; MCP is a typed pass-through wrapper.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>What is the WebMCP demo page?</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/webmcp">/webmcp</a> registers the same Canix MCP tools in the page via
        <code>document.modelContext.registerTool</code> (with
        <code>navigator.modelContext</code> as a fallback). People fill an opportunities table from
        those tools in a slide-out panel; agents in
        ChatGPT’s in-app browser or Chrome with
        <code>chrome://flags/#enable-webmcp-testing</code> discover the same tools through WebMCP. Canix does
        not sign or submit. Origin isolation is required — the page never relaxes
        <code>document.domain</code>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="faq-item">
      <h2>How do I test locally?</h2>
      <p>
        Run the protocol API and Caddy gateway from the monorepo, then point website env vars to
        your local gateway URL during development. Production API references in this site use
        <code>https://canix402-api.compx.io</code>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
